## ----chick-wts-mult,  out.width="100%", fig.height=5, fig.width=6, fig.cap= if (knitr::is_html_output()) "**Predicted Chick Weights With Different Confidence Intervals**. The display shows predicted chick weights with four different inferential confidence intervals -- 68% (the smallest one that works), 85% (the largest one that works), 76% (the middle of the range) and 78% (the one we identify as cognitively easiest).  The confidence levels are labelled in the strips at the top of the figure.  The two rows refer to two different tests of interest.  In the top row, the points that are significantly different, but have the confidence intervals closest to overlapping are plotted in black.  The smaller the confidence level, the easier it is to see that there is space between the upper bound for meatmeal and the lower bound for casein.  The bottom row considers a converse test -- two points that are not significantly different from each other, but whose confidence intervals are closest to not overlapping.  In this case, as the confidence level gets larger, it is easier to see the overlap in the confidence intervals and rightly identify the lack of statistical difference.  All else equal, values in the middle will tend to strike a balance between getting these two tests right." else "\\textbf{Predicted Chick Weights With Different Confidence Intervals}. The display shows predicted chick weights with four different inferential confidence intervals -- 68% (the smallest one that works), 85% (the largest one that works), 76% (the middle of the range) and 78% (the one we identify as cognitively easiest).  The confidence levels are labelled in the strips at the top of the figure.  The two rows refer to two different tests of interest.  In the top row, the points that are significantly different, but have the confidence intervals closest to overlapping are plotted in black.  The smaller the confidence level, the easier it is to see that there is space between the upper bound for meatmeal and the lower bound for casein.  The bottom row considers a converse test -- two points that are not significantly different from each other, but whose confidence intervals are closest to not overlapping.  In this case, as the confidence level gets larger, it is easier to see the overlap in the confidence intervals and rightly identify the lack of statistical difference.  All else equal, values in the middle will tend to strike a balance between getting these two tests right."----
